// BUG-ZYB-001 — a lambda that names a module alias, invoked from inside
// ANOTHER module.
//
// Three things have to meet before this breaks, which is why 614 corpus files
// never did it: a lambda, an alias inside it, and a call from another module's
// body. The tree-walker gave `undefined module alias: 'd'` while the register
// VM and the browser engine printed 10 — a divergence in a language whose gate
// declares zero.
//
// The cause was that a lambda carried no aliases of its own and read whichever
// ones the call site had. That is the same map only while the lambda is called
// from where it was written; hand it to a function in another module and it
// looks up `d` in that module's imports, where nothing of the sort exists.
//
// It matters more than its rarity suggests: wrapping in a lambda is the natural
// way around GAP-ZYB-005 (a module function is not a first-class value), so the
// workaround for a gap landed inside a bug, and programs needed a workaround
// for the workaround — a NAMED function doing the wrapping.
<# ./aplicador => a
<# ./doblador => d

env = (x) -> d::doble(x)

>> "en casa:   " env(5) ¶
>> "cruzando:  " a::aplicar(env, 5) ¶
